A global's address on ARM (docs/w4.md W4d). JavaScriptCore's offline
assembler writes the interpreter for ARM64 in one asm statement, and names
for each system how it reaches a global such as g_config: through Mach-O's
GOT on Darwin, ELF's GOT on Linux, and directly on Windows. A Kosmos image
is linked static at a fixed address and never relocated, so there is
nothing for a GOT to do: adrp and add, as on Windows.

--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/offlineasm/arm64.rb	2026-11-12 00:35:20.823378721 -0300
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/offlineasm/arm64.rb	2026-11-12 00:35:20.840308222 -0300
@@ -1468,8 +1468,10 @@
             $asm.puts "ldr #{operands[1].arm64Operand(:quad)}, [#{operands[1].arm64Operand(:quad)}, :got_lo12:#{operands[0].asmLabel}]"
             $asm.putStr("#endif")
 
-            # On Windows, use COFF-style addressing.
-            $asm.putStr("#elif OS(WINDOWS)")
+            # On Windows, use COFF-style addressing. On Kosmos too: an
+            # image is static and never relocated, so the address is
+            # reached directly, with no GOT.
+            $asm.putStr("#elif OS(WINDOWS) || OS(KOSMOS)")
 
             $asm.puts "adrp #{operands[1].arm64Operand(:quad)}, #{operands[0].asmLabel}"
             $asm.puts "add #{operands[1].arm64Operand(:quad)}, #{operands[1].arm64Operand(:quad)}, :lo12:#{operands[0].asmLabel}"
